{"name":"Bush Viper","hex":"#a0bcd0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bush","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A woody plant distinguished from a tree by its multiple stems and lower height, being usually less than six metres tall; a horticultural rather than strictly botanical category.","A shrub cut off, or a shrublike branch of a tree."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To branch thickly in the manner of a bush.","To set bushes for; to support with bushes."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bush"},{"term":"viper","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A venomous snake in the family Viperidae.","Any venomous snake."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/viper"}],"entries":[{"title":"Atheris","blocks":[{"text":"Atheris is a genus of vipers known as bush vipers. They are found only in tropical subsaharan Africa and many species have isolated and fragmented distributions due to their confinement to rain forests. Like all other vipers, they are venomous. In an example of convergent evolution, they show many similarities to the arboreal pit vipers of Asia and South America. Seventeen species are currently recognized."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Atheris_hispida.jpg/330px-Atheris_hispida.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atheris"},{"title":"Viper","blocks":[{"text":"Vipers are snakes in the family Viperidae, found in most parts of the world, except for Antarctica, Australia, Hawaii, Madagascar, Ireland, and various other isolated islands. All vipers are venomous, and have long, hinged fangs that permit deep envenomation of their prey. Three subfamilies are currently recognized. They are also known as viperids. The name \"viper\" is derived from the Latin word vipera, -ae, also meaning viper, possibly from vivus (\"living\") and parere, referring to the trait viviparity common in vipers like most of the species of Boidae. The earliest known vipers are believed to have diverged from the rest of the clade Caenophidia in the early Eocene."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Viperidae-01.jpg/330px-Viperidae-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viper"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Uzungwe_Mountain_Bush_Viper_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_353999490_on_19_April_2024.jpg/500px-Uzungwe_Mountain_Bush_Viper_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_353999490_on_19_April_2024.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Uzungwe_Mountain_Bush_Viper_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_353999490_on_19_April_2024.jpg/1280px-Uzungwe_Mountain_Bush_Viper_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_353999490_on_19_April_2024.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Uzungwe_Mountain_Bush_Viper_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_353999490_on_19_April_2024.jpg/500px-Uzungwe_Mountain_Bush_Viper_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_353999490_on_19_April_2024.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUzungwe_Mountain_Bush_Viper_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_353999490_on_19_April_2024.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Atheris_broadleyi_in_Cameroon.jpg/500px-Atheris_broadleyi_in_Cameroon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Atheris_broadleyi_in_Cameroon.jpg/1280px-Atheris_broadleyi_in_Cameroon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Atheris_br